# G070 — Web Browser with Tabs

*v2. See `browser.md` for prose: scope as the central design decision,
per-tab history as independent timelines, navigate truncates forward
(branching timeline), closed-tab recovery as orthogonal second undo
stack, active tab as a pointer not a state.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Multi-Topic Research Session

[research-session @topics
    IN ORDER TO
        open a tab per topic, walk top results within each tab
        bookmark pages that pass a relevance filter
        and synthesise a report from the browser-scoped bookmark list

    @browser <- [web[new-browser]]

    @tabs <- for @topic in @topics
        [web[open-tab
              {browser: @browser,
               url: [search[url-for {topic: @topic}]]}]]

    for @t in @tabs
        [web[switch-to {browser: @browser, id: @t}]]
        for @r in [search[top-results {n: 5}]]
            [web[navigate {browser: @browser, url: @r:url}]]
            @content <- [scrape {page: @r:url}]
            @content where relevance is greater than 0.7
                -> [web[add-bookmark {browser: @browser}]]

    @bookmarks <- [web[bookmarks {browser: @browser}]]
    @report    <- [synthesize {sources: @bookmarks}]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[web
    new-browser
    open-tab         {browser, url}
    close-tab        {browser, id}
    reopen-last-closed {browser}
    switch-to        {browser, id}
    navigate         {browser, url}           ;; truncates forward stack
    back             {browser}                 ;; || at-oldest
    forward          {browser}                 ;; || at-newest
    add-bookmark     {browser}
    bookmarks        {browser}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: scope as `where` state lives

[@browser
    IN ORDER TO
        keep every piece of state in exactly one scope
        so closing a tab never loses browser-wide data
        and the back stack of tab 1 is never altered by tab 2's navigation

    per-tab:     url, back stack, forward stack, scroll-position
    browser:     bookmarks, closed-tab stack, active-tab pointer
]

Getting the scope wrong is a design bug that manifests as user
surprise. "Why did closing this tab lose my bookmark?" — scope error.
"Why does Back go to a different tab?" — scope error.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: navigate truncates forward `where` timeline branches

[web[navigate {browser: @b, url: @u}]
    @active-tab
        where forward stack is non-empty
            -> discard forward stack (user has branched onto a new path)
    push current url onto back stack
    set current url to @u
]

Same discipline every editor undo and every VCS has: redo is lost
the moment you branch. The closed-tab stack is the workaround —
different scope, different lifecycle.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @b  <- [web[new-browser]]
    @t1 <- [web[open-tab {browser: @b, url: "https://a"}]]
    [web[navigate {browser: @b, url: "https://b"}]]
    [web[navigate {browser: @b, url: "https://c"}]]

    [web[back {browser: @b}]]
    -> @t1 current url                         ;; -> "https://b"

    [web[navigate {browser: @b, url: "https://d"}]]
    -> forward stack of @t1                     ;; -> [] (truncated)

    @t2 <- [web[open-tab {browser: @b, url: "https://x"}]]
    [web[close-tab {browser: @b, id: @t2}]]
    [web[reopen-last-closed {browser: @b}]]
    -> @t2 current url                          ;; -> "https://x" (recovered)
]
